  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAfter a cold morning temperatures will moderate today, as 850 \ntemperatures climb to +6 to +9 C ahead of a trough. With plenty \nof sun south of the mountains temperatures should be able to \nfully mix into the low to mid 60s. Clouds move in early over the\nmountains so expect temperatures there to top out in the 50s \nfor the most part. These warmer 850 temperatures are going to \nmake for a comparatively warmer night with temperatures only \nfalling into the 40s area wide. A few showers are possible \ntonight mainly over the mountains as the front approaches the \narea. \n\nThe 07/00Z CAMs suggest the more organized band of showers at \nthe New Hampshire boarder in the early hours of Thursday \nmorning, moving into western Maine around day break. There is \ndecent agreement amongst the CAMs that these produce a general \ntenth to a quarter of an inch for most of the area, with \nsouthern New Hampshire being the most likely to miss out or only\nget a few hundredths. There is also some suggestion that a few \nlocations may see greater than a quarter of an inch if they get \nunder a more robust shower. Dry air quickly fills in behind the \nfront so shower activity looks to become greatly reduced by the \nafternoon hours with areas south of the mountains breaking out \ninto full sun. Temperatures here should be able to climb into \nthe upper 60s and low 70s. In the north where it will stay a bit\ncloudier and cooler air will work in first, temperatures likely\nonly top out in the low to mid-60s. Continued troughing aloft \nand northwest flow will also provide the forcing to sustain \nshowers in the mountains through Friday night. Temperatures \nThursday night will only be slightly cooler than tonight.\n\nTroughing slowly exits the area on Friday, but areas south of \nthe mountains should remain dry and mostly sunny. 850 mb \ntemperatures drop back down to -1 to +3 C, which would suggest \nhighs in the low to mid-60s. In the mountains where clouds and \nshowers linger, expect to top out in the 50s. As high pressure \nbegins to build into the region Friday night, we could see an \nopportunity for radiational cooling once again pushing lows into\nthe 30s for most of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will allow for a dry and relatively mild weekend with \ndaytime highs on Saturday into the lower to middle 60s south of the \nmountains before potentially reaching the lower 70s on Sunday as \nflow becomes southwesterly. Forecast uncertainty then increases \ntowards early next week as an approaching frontal boundary and area \nof low pressure approaches from the south and west. There is \ngenerally moderate confidence that rain will occur sometime between \nthe Sunday night to Monday night timeframe, but potential rainfall \namounts are of much lower confidence. For now, the areas most likely \nto receive beneficial rainfall for the ongoing drought conditions \nlook to be southern and coastal communities but a lot can change \ngiven we are still several days out. After a mild start to the week, \ncooler temperatures are likely to arrive by late week.",
